Numerical integration
---------------------

The integration rule depends on the type of integration (inttype), the shape
of the element (globalshape) and the integration rule (intrule and intrule2).

The meaning of intrule depends on inttype and globalshape, as follows:
  inttype=0: standard Gauss-Legendre (using analytical expressions)
     - triangle/tetrahedron/prism: number of Gauss points
       (prism: base triangle only).
       possibilities: triangle: 1, 3, 6, 7, 12
                      tetrahedron: 1, 4, 8, 15, 24
     - line/quadrilateral/hexahedron: number of Gauss points in one dimension
       (maximum: 5)
  inttype=1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
     - line/quadrilateral/hexahedron: number of Gauss points in one dimension
  inttype=2: mapped Gauss (from quad->triangle, hexahedron->pyramid)
     - triangle: number of Gauss points in one dimension
     - pyramid: number of Gauss points in one dimension (quad base only)
  inttype=3: standard Gauss-Legendre (using tabulated or numerical results)
     - triangle/tetrahedron/prism/pyramid: maximum polynomial order p for exact
       integration (prism: base triangle only).
       (maximum: 21 (triangle), 14 (tetrahedron), 10 (pyramid)).
     - line/quadrilateral/hexahedron: number of Gauss points in one dimension
       (maximum: 20).

For the following cases:
  - prisms
  - pyramids with mapped Gauss (inttype=2)
a secondary integration rule (intrule2) needs to be given, specifying the
number of Gauss points in height direction.

NOTE: inttype can be set directly (default) or indirectly by specification
      of the integration order.
NOTE: alternative integration rules are available by setting the variable
      GAUSS_RULE in the module limits_m. See the source file src/limits.f90.
NOTE: All values of inttype can be used in a composite integration rule
      with nsubint>1.
